course: dictionary
lesson: 11921
title: Calamity and context
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A calamity is a terrible event that causes great damage, loss or suffering. Floods, earthquakes and storms can all be calamities.
explain: «The flood was a calamity for the village, and many homes were lost.» The clues are great damage and lost homes, so a calamity is something really bad.
explain: Sometimes people use calamity as a joke. «It's a calamity: I've lost my pencil!» A lost pencil is not a disaster, so the speaker makes a small mess sound big. Check the clues: is anyone really hurt, or is it funny?
fact: Calamity is a noun: one calamity, two calamities.
fact: «Calamity» means a terrible event that causes great damage, loss or suffering.
fact: «Calamity» can also mean a small accident or mess, said in a joking way.
fact: A natural calamity is a disaster caused by nature, such as an earthquake or a flood.
fact: Calamity Jane is a nickname for someone who has many accidents.
exercise: «The flood was a calamity for the village.» What does calamity mean here? | *A terrible event that causes great damage, loss or suffering | A small, funny mess | A new kind of bicycle | A sunny day at the beach
exercise: «It's a calamity: I've lost my pencil!» Is this serious or joking? | *Joking, a small mess made to sound big | A weather warning on the news | A kind of animal | A real disaster that hurt many people
exercise: «The earthquake was a natural calamity.» What kind of event is a natural calamity? | A type of cake | A school trip to the museum | A game played outdoors at break time | *An event caused by nature, like an earthquake
test: «A great calamity struck the kingdom, and the crops were lost.» What does calamity mean in this sentence? | A royal party with music | *A terrible event that causes great damage | A small mistake in a sum | A new road built through the hills
test: Zoe says, «I've spilled my drink, what a calamity!» Is she describing a real disaster? | *No, it is a small mess, said in a joking way | Yes, an earthquake has shaken the house | Yes, a flood has destroyed her town | No, she is talking about the weather
exam: Mia says calamity always means a huge disaster. Is she right? | *No, it can also mean a small, joking mess | No, it means a party or a celebration | Yes, it always means a huge disaster | Yes, but only in stories and never in real life
